+# Keep post revisions on every edit
+
+## Context
+
+Saving the edit form overwrote a post for good. Authors lost published
+posts by pasting the wrong draft over them and had no way back.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Every successful edit copies the title, excerpt and body the post had
+  *before* the save into `post_revisions`. Creating a post keeps nothing.
+- All content writes go through `Post.revise()`, which keeps the revision
+  and updates the post in one transaction: a save that fails leaves no
+  revision behind.
+- Restoring a revision is itself an edit through `Post.revise()`. The
+  replaced version becomes a new revision and the restored one stays, so
+  history only grows.
+- A save that changes nothing still keeps a revision; revisions are not
+  deduplicated.
+- History is private to the post's author. `PostPolicy.viewRevisions` and
+  `restoreRevision` are separate from `update`, so opening editing to other
+  roles later does not open the history with it.
+- A revision is looked up within its post, so a revision id of another
+  post answers 404 even when the same author owns both.
+- Deleting a post deletes its revisions in the same transaction. The
+  foreign key also declares `ON DELETE CASCADE`, but SQLite only enforces
+  it with foreign keys switched on, so the code does not rely on it.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- An author can undo any overwrite, including an accidental restore.
+- `post_revisions` grows by one full copy of the content per edit and is
+  never pruned. Pruning and side-by-side comparison are out of scope.
+- Code that updates a post's content with `Post.update()` directly
+  bypasses the history; use `Post.revise()`.
